WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:14.890
At an executive ground-transportation company, a reservation request arrived three ways: a vendor's web form, a forwarded thread, or a few lines of prose. The price that came back had to be one a person could trust.

2
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:29.980
On 22 June 2026 the client sent four real inbound examples with the instruction to respond to any of them. That made the reading the hard part and the arithmetic the part that could not be allowed to move.

3
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:46.760
The queue on a keyless local copy at the pinned commit, with invented requests the product's own code priced: a merged duplicate marked ready on a flat-rate route, a held round trip, a forward-only market, and two requests a person must read.

4
00:00:47.900 --> 00:01:02.530
The price is a pure function of typed inputs, with every rate a literal in one file. Any quote built from a field a model read is marked for a person, so a deterministic price is only as right as the fields behind it.

5
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:18.210
The Quote Tester: a trip entered by hand, priced by the same engine, and explained line by line with a running total, a margin band and the alert that the 200-mile minimum applied. Explain the number before trusting it.

6
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:32.730
Counts with dates, from the repository's own log and a test run at the last commit. No revenue, bookings or hours figure exists, and none is claimed; no post-fix cost figure exists either.

7
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:51.140
The dashboard over the invented rows, with the product's own sentence that auto-send stays off. The 0.90 threshold is a constant and a helper that nothing calls; the only send route requires a person's action and is dry unless its flag enables delivery.

8
00:01:52.200 --> 00:02:04.190
Computed dead legs, reply stripping and forward merging were built locally after the last commit and never committed or deployed. The project was sunset in September 2026.
